Let’s write **optimized versions** of the sorting algorithms—especially **Bubble Sort**, which benefits most from optimization.

---

## 🧠 Why optimize?

- Reduce unnecessary passes.
- For example: if the array is already sorted, we should **exit early**.
- This saves time from **O(n²)** to **O(n)** in best case.

---

## ✅ Optimized Bubble Sort (with Early Exit)

```c
#include <stdio.h>

void optimizedBubbleSort(int arr[], int n) {
    for(int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        int swapped = 0;  // Flag to detect any swap

        for(int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
            if(arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                // Swap
                int temp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
                arr[j + 1] = temp;
                swapped = 1;  // Mark that we swapped
            }
        }

        // If no two elements were swapped, array is already sorted
        if(swapped == 0)
            break;
    }
}
```

### 🔍 Example Behavior:
For input `[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]`:
- Normal Bubble Sort → still runs full passes (slow)
- Optimized → stops after first pass (fast 🚀)

---

## ✅ Optimized Insertion Sort (Already efficient)

Insertion Sort is already quite efficient for nearly sorted data.
- Still, we can avoid unnecessary assignments by checking if shifting is needed before doing it.
- Most optimization here is **algorithmic behavior**, not code-level.

### Already optimized (simple but fast for small/sorted arrays):
```c
void insertionSort(int arr[], int n) {
    for(int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
        int key = arr[i];
        int j = i - 1;

        if (arr[j] <= key)
            continue; // No shifting needed

        while(j >= 0 && arr[j] > key) {
            arr[j + 1] = arr[j];
            j--;
        }
        arr[j + 1] = key;
    }
}
```

---

## ✅ Optimized Selection Sort?

Selection Sort has **no big optimization gain**:
- It always looks for the minimum in the unsorted part.
- Doesn’t benefit from early exit like bubble or insertion.

BUT: You can **skip unnecessary swaps**:

```c
void selectionSort(int arr[], int n) {
    for(int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        int minIndex = i;

        for(int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
            if(arr[j] < arr[minIndex])
                minIndex = j;
        }

        // Only swap if needed
        if(minIndex != i) {
            int temp = arr[i];
            arr[i] = arr[minIndex];
            arr[minIndex] = temp;
        }
    }
}
```

