🧠 **Straight answer**:  
For learning and writing clean code — **recursive Quick Sort** is **easier and more natural**.

---

## 🔁 Why Recursive Quick Sort is Easier

| Feature                  | Recursive                         | Iterative                        |
|--------------------------|-----------------------------------|----------------------------------|
| 🧠 Intuition              | Matches the **divide & conquer** idea | Simulates recursion manually     |
| 📏 Code size              | Short and clean                   | Longer, needs stack handling     |
| 🛠️ Uses                   | Natural recursion                 | Manual stack (array-based)       |
| 🧑‍💻 Beginner-friendly      | ✅ Yes                              | ❌ No (harder to trace/debug)     |
| ⚡ Performance            | Almost same as iterative          | Almost same                     |

---

## ✅ Recommendation for You (Student-Level):

Stick to **recursive Quick Sort** for exams, understanding, and coding interviews **unless**:
- You are hitting **stack overflow** (very deep recursion)
- You’re working in **embedded or memory-constrained systems**

---

## 🧪 Here's a comparison:

### ✅ Recursive Quick Sort (Easy)
```c
void quickSort(int arr[], int low, int high) {
    if (low < high) {
        int pivotIndex = partition(arr, low, high);
        quickSort(arr, low, pivotIndex - 1);   // Sort left
        quickSort(arr, pivotIndex + 1, high);  // Sort right
    }
}
```

---

### 🧱 Iterative Quick Sort (Harder)

You simulate the call stack manually:

```c
void iterativeQuickSort(int arr[], int low, int high) {
    int stack[high - low + 1];
    int top = -1;

    // Push initial values
    stack[++top] = low;
    stack[++top] = high;

    while (top >= 0) {
        high = stack[top--];
        low = stack[top--];

        int pivotIndex = partition(arr, low, high);

        // If there are elements on left of pivot, push them
        if (pivotIndex - 1 > low) {
            stack[++top] = low;
            stack[++top] = pivotIndex - 1;
        }

        // If there are elements on right of pivot, push them
        if (pivotIndex + 1 < high) {
            stack[++top] = pivotIndex + 1;
            stack[++top] = high;
        }
    }
}
```

⚠️ Needs:
- Stack array
- Manual index tracking
- More prone to bugs

---

## 👑 Final Verdict:

👉 **Use Recursive Quick Sort for learning, exams, and interviews**  
👉 Use Iterative only when recursion is not allowed or stack overflow is possible

